The flow of authentication:

1. http://localhost:8200/ -> response.sendRedirect("/oauth2/authorization/google"); -> User clicks “Login with Google”.

2. Frontend/browser is redirected to Google, user consents.

3. Google redirects back to your backend with a code; Spring Security completes OAuth2 login and gives you an OAuth2User (or OidcUser) with email.
OAuth2LoginSuccessHandler.onAuthenticationSuccess(reads the user from db if it exists or creates a new one & sends back the token
http://localhost:8200/login/oauth2/code/google?state=......email+profile+https... userinfo.profile+openid&authuser=0&prompt=none) read point 4

4. In a custom OAuth2 success handler / custom OAuth2UserService:

    Check if a local User exists by email.

    If not, create and save a new User row (first login).

    Build a Spring Security UserDetails (or your custom implementation) from that local user.

    Generate a JWT for your own API (subject = email, roles, userId, etc.).

5. Send the JWT to the client (e.g. in redirect URL, or in a JSON response for SPA).

6. For all subsequent API calls, the client sends Authorization: Bearer <jwt>.

7. A JWT filter validates the token and uses UserDetailsService.loadUserByUsername(email) to load your local user and set the Authentication in the security context.

